WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.386
When a laptop joins a network, it may know its
hardware address but not its local IP address,

2
00:00:07.386 --> 00:00:11.054
subnet mask, default gateway, DNS servers, or
lease policy.

3
00:00:11.054 --> 00:00:17.591
It cannot simply send a normal unicast request
to a DHCP server because it does not yet know

4
00:00:17.591 --> 00:00:21.223
where that server is—or even which subnet it
belongs to.

5
00:00:21.223 --> 00:00:29.548
DHCP bootstraps this uncertainty with a short
exchange often remembered as DORA: Discover,

6
00:00:29.548 --> 00:00:32.109
Offer, Request, and Acknowledgment.

7
00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:39.232
The client sends DHCPDISCOVER as a broadcast
so any available server or relay on the local

8
00:00:39.232 --> 00:00:40.988
network can receive it.

9
00:00:40.988 --> 00:00:46.652
The message includes a transaction identifier
that helps the client match later responses.

10
00:00:46.652 --> 00:00:53.422
If the server is on another subnet, a DHCP
relay listens locally and forwards the request

11
00:00:53.422 --> 00:00:57.654
toward the configured server, adding
information about the client's network.

12
00:00:57.654 --> 00:01:02.498
This matters because routers normally do not
forward broadcasts.

13
00:01:02.498 --> 00:01:09.161
The relay provides controlled cross-subnet
reach without placing a DHCP server on every

14
00:01:09.161 --> 00:01:09.673
VLAN.

15
00:01:09.794 --> 00:01:16.206
One or more servers may respond with DHCPOFFER
messages containing a proposed address, lease

16
00:01:16.206 --> 00:01:18.038
time, and configuration options.

17
00:01:18.038 --> 00:01:24.444
The client chooses an offer and broadcasts
DHCPREQUEST identifying the selected server

18
00:01:24.444 --> 00:01:25.512
and address.

19
00:01:25.512 --> 00:01:31.649
Broadcasting the choice informs the winning
server to proceed and allows other servers to

20
00:01:31.649 --> 00:01:33.402
withdraw their pending offers.

21
00:01:33.402 --> 00:01:38.103
This is a coordination step, not merely a
repeat of the request.

22
00:01:38.103 --> 00:01:43.853
Packet captures reveal the selected-server and
requested-address options that make the

23
00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:45.421
client's intent explicit.

24
00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:50.506
The selected server returns DHCPACK,
confirming the lease and options.

25
00:01:50.506 --> 00:01:54.568
The client configures the interface and enters
the bound state.

26
00:01:54.568 --> 00:01:56.196
A lease is temporary.

27
00:01:56.196 --> 00:02:02.146
At the first renewal timer, the client usually
unicasts a request to the original server.

28
00:02:02.146 --> 00:02:08.266
If that fails, a later rebinding timer allows
broadcast requests to any server.

29
00:02:08.266 --> 00:02:13.513
If the lease expires without renewal, the
address must no longer be used.

30
00:02:13.513 --> 00:02:19.607
These timers let address pools be reused while
keeping stable configuration during normal

31
00:02:19.607 --> 00:02:20.076
operation.

32
00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.790
Troubleshooting follows the exchange.

33
00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.446
Confirm link and VLAN first.

34
00:02:25.446 --> 00:02:31.177
Capture whether Discover leaves the client,
whether the relay forwards it, whether an

35
00:02:31.177 --> 00:02:34.704
Offer returns, and whether Request and
Acknowledgment complete.

36
00:02:34.704 --> 00:02:40.889
Then inspect the scope for exhaustion, option
errors, reservations, and lease conflicts.

37
00:02:40.889 --> 00:02:48.063
Also ask who is answering: a rogue DHCP server
can supply a malicious gateway or DNS setting.

38
00:02:48.063 --> 00:02:53.532
DORA is therefore both an availability
workflow and a trust boundary.

39
00:02:53.532 --> 00:03:00.258
Seeing each packet in context turns 'the
network is down' into a testable sequence of

40
00:03:00.258 --> 00:03:00.706
hypotheses.
